{
 "id": "P08",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "A 0 to 100 score gives the monitor more cutoffs to choose from",
 "section": "results",
 "order": 80,
 "caption": "Each dot is a usable cutoff: honest code audited (across) against backdoors caught (up). Grey is the default 0 to 10 prompt, violet the same monitor asked for 0 to 100. Sonnet has 4 cutoffs at 5% or below on 0 to 10 and 5 on 0 to 100.",
 "notes": [
  "This explains the wide intervals in P05 only in part. Those come mostly from how few honest programs sit above the cutoff (5 at 1%), and a wider scale doesn't add honest programs.",
  "The scale isn't purely cosmetic: Marnick measured it raising AUC by about +14 points for GPT-6 Luna and +8.5 for Gemma 4, but under a point for Sonnet and Haiku.",
  "MonitoringBench saw 0 to 100 scores collapse at the top (honest code hitting 100). A wider scale helps when honest scores pile up low, as ours do, and can hurt when they pile up high."
 ],
 "height": 440,
